          %title Absolute differential forms
          %h1#absolute_differential_forms Absolute differential forms
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definitions"} Definitions
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            It's well known that one can integrate a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            on an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} oriented
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifold"} submanifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+pseudoform"} differential pseudoform
            on an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudoriented"} pseudoriented
            (transversely oriented) submanifold. But in classical differential geometry, one also sees forms that can be integrated on
            %em unoriented
            submanifolds.
          %p
            I call these
            %em absolute
            forms. The term ‘absolute’ suggests a lack of additional required structure, in this case some sort of orientation on the domain of integration. It also suggests
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+value"} absolute value
          %p
            The main theorem of absolute forms is that, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is a (pseudo)-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            \-form and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a (pseudo)-oriented
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo |
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi R
                    %mi ω
                    %mo |
                  %mo ≤
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\left| \int_R \omega \right|} \leq \int_{|R|} {|\omega|} ,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|\omega|}
            is an absolute
            = succeed "-form" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |{R}|
            is simply
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            with its (pseudo)-orientation ignored, and the absolute value on the left is the ordinary absolute value of scalars. This theorem also applies if we start with an absolute
            = succeed "-form" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            starts out unoriented and so is the same as
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|R|}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham
            chain (a formal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+combination"} linear combination
            of appropriately oriented submanifolds), we also take absolute values of the formal coefficients in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|R|}
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+manifold"} differentiable manifold
            (or similar sort of space), and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            (typically
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi p
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq p \leq n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exterior+differential+form"} form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            that assigns a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            (or whatever is the relevant sort of scalar)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_c(v_1,\ldots,v_p)
            to a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and a
            = succeed "-" do
